{
  "name": "How to Prevent Leaks in Your Shower Tile Project",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Preventing leaks in your shower isn't just about setting tile; it's fundamentally about a robust waterproofing system. Many homeowners mistakenly believe that water-resistant drywall is waterproof, but it's not enough for a wet environment like a shower. The first crucial step is to ensure your shower walls and base are structurally sound. Any movement can compromise the waterproofing. Next, you absolutely need a dedicated waterproofing system. This often involves applying a waterproof membrane or liquid sealant over the substrate before any tile goes down. HH Remodeling LLC always emphasizes this step, as it's the core defense against hidden water damage. You also need to confirm that your shower pan has been properly flood tested for at least 24 hours *before* any mortar bed or floor tile is installed. Using the correct adhesive rated for wet areas is also key. Skipping these critical waterproofing details is a recipe for disaster and is precisely why getting a professional involved in shower tile installation is so important.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
